Title: THE PRODIGAL SON
Source: Luke 15:1-32, in the Tolkien voice
Draft date: v3, 20 October 2026
Notes: Written from PRODIGAL_V3_DESIGN_NOTE.md. About 48 minutes by the scene budget. The 45-minute target is soft: up to an hour is allowed (owner, 20 Oct). Every line from Luke 15 is the approved Tolkien rendering, checked word for word by check_v3_screenplay.py. Other Scripture: Numbers 6:24-26, Deuteronomy 21:18-21, Joshua 1:9, Ruth 4:11, Genesis 25:23 and 33:4. Made on H3 Ref2VA from assets only. The cold open is opening C, the lynching (owner, 21 Oct 2026: "no more ab testing eeryonne agrees c is the right one"). Openings A (the town) and B (the farm) are retired; the three-way draft is kept as "... - with openings A B C.fountain".

===

EXT. EDGE OF A FIELD ON THE ROAD WEST - NOON

Black. Shouting. A sound like a fist on a door.

White noon. Close on two brothers, two young men in good tunics, beating a young man curled in the dust under a field wall, hired men crowding in around them.

SMASH CUT TO:

EXT. HILLSIDE ABOVE THE LAKE - LATE AFTERNOON #1#

A low sun through dusty haze. An old olive on a terraced slope above a pale lake; the far shore is a line of hills going grey.

Thirty-one people, counting the four under the olive, sit in loose groups among the trunks and stones. Fishermen with cracked hands. Women with veils pulled close. A few publicans in good cloth sitting awkwardly on the dirt. Bread passes from hand to hand. A child chases a lizard between the groups. A donkey is tethered at a low stone wall that runs to an open gate.

JESUS sits on a limestone ledge under the olive. He tears a barley round and hands half of it on. Dust in the creases of his palms.

At his knee sits HEZRON (40s), a publican, his fine linen gone dull, a lead seal on a cord at his chest. His daughter TIRZAH (8) holds his sleeve. Near them sits the SHEPHERD BOY (17), staff across his knees, a sling at his rope belt.

Outside the gate, in the shade of the grove, stand two Pharisees: JOTHAM (40s), black headcloth, a wax tablet in his hand, and ELEAZAR (50s), striped mantle, arms folded. They do not come in.

ELEAZAR
A tax-gatherer at his knee. Eating his bread.

JOTHAM
His own father will not say his name.

ELEAZAR
Nor should any man in Israel.

Eleazar lifts his voice, for the whole slope to hear.

ELEAZAR
This man receives sinners, and eats with them.

Heads turn. Hezron's hand stops on the bread.

Jesus does not answer the slope. He turns on the ledge toward the gate, toward the two men in the shade, and speaks to them.

JESUS
Which man among you, having a hundred sheep, if he lose one of them, would not leave the ninety and nine in the wilderness,

JESUS
and go after the one that is lost, until he find it?

Nobody at the gate answers. It is the boy who answers, simply, as if it were obvious.

SHEPHERD BOY
Any shepherd would.

A ripple of laughter runs across the slope. Jesus smiles at the boy, then turns back to the gate.

JESUS
And when he has found it, he lays it upon his shoulders, rejoicing.

JESUS
And coming home, he calls together his friends and his neighbours, and says to them, Rejoice with me, for I have found my sheep that was lost.

JESUS
I say to you, that even so there shall be joy in heaven over one sinner who repents, more than over ninety and nine righteous men who need no repentance.

Eleazar's jaw tightens. Jotham looks down at his tablet. The stylus has not moved.

The breeze off the lake lifts the veils. Jesus looks at the bread in his hands, and then at the gate again.

JESUS
A certain man had two sons.

EXT. EDGE OF A FIELD ON THE ROAD WEST - NOON #1A#

Black. The shouting again, closer, and the blows.

White noon, and full chaos. Close on two brothers of a foreign house beating a YOUNG MAN in the dust at the edge of a road, under a wall of piled field stones: the ELDER SON (25), sunburnt, his good Greek tunic dark with sweat, and the YOUNGER SON (19), quick on his feet, landing kick after kick. Around them HIRED MEN in work clothes kick and beat at him too, a dozen hands grabbing at his rags. The young man has his arms wrapped over his head: bare cut feet, matted hair, cheeks hollow, skin burnt dark by the road, a shepherd's sling wrapped around his fist. Over the wall, stubble and a last strip of standing barley, green. Across the road the land is burnt brown and dead, and it falls away in a steep slope of scree and thorn toward a dry wadi far below.

HIRED MAN
Hold him!

SECOND MAN
His arms! Get his arms!

A heel comes down on the young man's hand. He screams and twists, his rags tear away in their fists, and he is loose.

He runs: down the road, barefoot, staggering, the men shouting behind him.

The elder son stoops, pulls a stone from the wall, and throws.

The stone takes the young man on the back of the head. His legs go. He pitches off the edge of the road and over the slope.

THROUGH HIS EYES

Sky, scree, sky, thorns, the world turning over and over as he rolls.

FLASH. Pigs rooting at a trough in the dust; his own hand drifting toward the husks.

Stones. Sky. Stones.

FLASH. A purse tipped over an open palm; a single coin falls out.

FLASH. A man at the bend of a country road, holding out his own cloak.

Thorns tearing past.

FLASH. An old man's hand, shielding a new flame at a farm gate at dusk.

He crashes into the thorn scrub at the bottom, and the branches close over him.

EXT. TOP OF THE SLOPE - CONTINUOUS

The men stand at the edge of the road, breathing hard, looking down. The slope falls away to a sea of grey thorn. Nothing moves in it.

HIRED MAN
I do not see him.

SECOND MAN
It is a long way down.

The elder son looks down a moment longer, and wipes his hands on his thighs.

ELDER SON
Let the thorns have him.

They turn back up the road, the elder son's arm round his younger brother's shoulders.

EXT. FOOT OF THE SLOPE - THORN SCRUB - CONTINUOUS

Deep in the thorns, half hidden, he lies on his side: his brow split open by the stones of the slope, his face a mask of blood and dust, his trodden hand swelling. His breath drags in and out, heavy and wet.

THROUGH HIS EYES

Thorn branches against a white sky, blurred and swimming. The voices on the road above go far away.

A shape comes between him and the sun: a man in a rust-red mantle, bending down through the thorns toward him, too blurred to have a face.

ELIAB (V.O.)
Asher... Asher...

His eyelids sink, and sink.

ELIAB (V.O.)
My son...

Black.

INT. ELIAB'S HOUSE - BEFORE DAWN #2#

ELIAB (O.S.)
Asher.

Dark. ASHER (20) jolts awake on his mat, gasping, his hands flying to his face. Whole. Young. No blood. Curls flattened on one side, still in the tunic he slept in: undyed linen with a purple border at the neck, the sleeves and the hem, the one rich colour in the house. A shepherd's sling hangs on a peg above his head.

ELIAB (65), a rust-red mantle over undyed wool, a carnelian signet on his hand, fills the doorway with a clay lamp, the dark yard behind him.

ELIAB
The flock goes out at first light. Your flock.

He goes. Asher stares into the dark a moment while his breathing slows. Then he turns over and pulls the blanket over his head.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - THRESHING FLOOR - BEFORE DAWN

A farm on a terrace above a valley still full of mist, oven smoke rising straight up, the village roofs below and the road east a pale thread winding down toward far hills. Somewhere in the dark house, the millstones are grinding.

Six HIRED MEN come up the path from the village, cloaks over their heads against the cold. Eliab gives each man a round of bread from a basket.

ELIAB
Bread first. No man works my floor hungry.

On the floor, dressed like them, AMOS (40) is already at the flail, his beard grey at the chin, the back of his tunic dark with sweat. MICAH (70s), the old steward, a knotted tally-cord at his belt, watches him from beside Eliab.

MICAH
I remember when there was only you and that boy on this floor, before sunrise. No hired men in those days.

Eliab looks at Amos, and says nothing.

INT. ELIAB'S HOUSE - DAWN

Grey light through the open door. MIRIAM (60s), floured to the elbows, shakes the sleeping shape on the mat.

MIRIAM
Every morning since your mother died I have woken you, and I am not tired of it yet. Up.

Eyes still shut, Asher catches her floured hands and kisses them. Miriam shakes the flour from her fingers, trying not to smile. He gets up and stumbles out of the door.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - FOLD - CONTINUOUS

The flock bunched in the fold, bells knocking. DAN (12) waits at the fold gate with a stick.

Asher, squinting, puts two fingers in his mouth and whistles. His DOG, lean and brown, is off the wall and working before the sound has ended, turning the flock out through the gate in one easy curve.

ASHER
The dog knows the way better than I do.

Dan puts two fingers in his mouth and blows. Only air. Asher, yawning, folds the boy's fingers the right way and holds them to Dan's lips. Dan blows again, and the whistle comes; out on the slope the dog's head turns. Dan's face splits in a grin.

Dan runs after the flock. Asher goes back inside, lies down on his mat, and pulls the blanket over his head.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DUSK

Dust lit gold as the flock comes in. Micah at the gatepost, his thumb moving along the tally-cord. Eliab beside him. Amos comes up from the floor.

Asher strolls down from the hill, yawning, the dog at his heel.

MICAH
One short.

ELIAB
The ewe with the torn ear.

Silence. Eliab looks at Asher. Asher grins and opens his mouth. Before he can speak:

AMOS
(through his teeth, looking at neither of them)
I sent the dog out with them.

Eliab looks at Amos a long moment, and lets it stand. Then he turns to Asher. His voice is quiet.

ELIAB
The dog cannot count, Asher. That is why I gave the flock to you, and not to the dog.

ASHER
It is one ewe, Father.

ELIAB
It was one of a hundred.

Eliab takes the clay lamp from the niche in the gatepost and lights it from the coal Miriam holds out on a shard. His old hand cups the new flame against the wind. He sets the lamp inside a pierced clay lantern and goes out of the gate into the falling dark. He tells nobody to follow.

Amos watches him go. Asher watches him go. Neither of them moves.

EXT. THE VALLEY - NIGHT

A huge black valley. One small lamp moving across it, very slowly, smaller and smaller. Jackals somewhere in the far hills.

EXT. VILLAGE - FIRE BY THE WELL - NIGHT

Down in the village, young men and travellers sit around a fire by the well. Sparks go up. Asher sits closest, the firelight on his face, the young men hanging on his words. He squares his shoulders, drops his voice and lifts an imaginary lamp.

ASHER
(as Eliab)
The flock goes out at first light. Your flock.

The young men roar. A TRAVELLER, his camel kneeling behind him, laughs with them, then leans in and talks with his hands.

TRAVELLER
In the cities beyond the Jordan they hang lamps in the streets, and they burn all night long. No one asks who pays for the oil.

Asher stares into the fire.

Far up the hill behind him, unnoticed, one small lamp moves along the black slope.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - LATE NIGHT

Asher comes up the path from the village fire, laughing to himself at something, and stops at the gatepost. The niche is empty and dark.

Below him a lantern is coming up the hill, the only light on the black slope. It hangs from Eliab's belt. He has the ewe laid across his shoulders, her legs gripped in both fists, his knuckles raw. His rust-red mantle is ripped at the shoulder and streaked with thorn scratches. His breath is loud and uneven, and under it he is singing, low. The ewe's head hangs. One ear is torn.

The dog barks once. The house door opens: Micah with a lamp, Miriam behind him, Dan rubbing his eyes, servants pulling on cloaks.

Eliab reaches the gatepost. He takes the lamp out of the lantern and sets it back in its niche. The flame steadies. Moths find it. Then, in front of them all, he lifts the ewe clear of his shoulders and holds her out.

She is in Asher's arms, filthy, thorns in her fleece, her weight dragging his arms down.

ELIAB
She is yours to keep.

Dan grins. A servant laughs. Asher's shoulders rise and stay high. He opens his mouth, and shuts it.

Eliab turns to them all.

ELIAB
Rejoice with me, for I have found my sheep that was lost.

He goes in, and the household goes in with him. Yellow lamplight spills across the threshold. A bench scrapes. A second lamp is lit. Someone laughs.

Asher carries the ewe to the fold, sets her down among the others, and lowers the bar. His palm stays flat on the wood.

Through the open door: Eliab, still in the torn mantle, pouring wine from a skin into Micah's cup, his shoulders shaking with laughter. Miriam tearing a round of bread. The dog under the bench.

In the doorway, half in the light and half in the dark, stands Amos, his sleeves still rolled to the elbow. He does not step over the threshold.

Eliab sees him. He takes a round of bread from the table, comes to the door, and puts it into Amos's hand.

ELIAB
Come in, son. She was lost, and is found.

Amos takes the bread. He does not come in. Eliab goes back to the light.

Asher comes to the door beside his brother, out of his father's hearing.

ASHER
(low, half a laugh, not a happy one)
I cannot even lose a sheep without him finding it.

Amos looks at him, and goes out into the dark with the bread.

Asher lies down on his mat against the outside wall, under the eaves. He does not pull the blanket up. His eyes stay open. Inside, the laughter and the clink of a cup go on. The gate lamp burns.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - THRESHING FLOOR - EVENING #3#

Low sun. The evening wind. Amos winnows alone, tossing forkfuls of threshed barley into the air; the grain falls and the chaff blows away, gold in the light. Across the valley, other floors are working.

Asher comes up and takes the second fork. Amos looks at him.

Asher plants his feet exactly like Amos, squares his shoulders exactly like Amos, and grunts exactly like Amos.

ASHER
(as Amos)
There is barley still to thresh. I will be up before you. I always am.

The corner of Amos's mouth moves, almost a smile. It's gone.

AMOS
You have never held a fork right in your life.

ASHER
I held it right for a moment. You were not looking.

They toss together for a while. Then Asher stops.

ASHER
I am asking him for my portion. Tonight.

Amos stops. Chaff drifts between them.

ASHER
Come with me. Ask for yours. Beyond the Jordan the streets are lit all night, and no one counts the oil.

AMOS
At your age I had threshed ten summers. You have slept through all of yours.

Asher grins, and tosses a forkful high, as if it were a joke.

AMOS
A son receives his portion when he bears his father to the tomb. Not before.

The grin goes.

AMOS
He fed half this valley through the bad year. They will say his son buried him alive.

ASHER
He went out in the dark for one ewe and came back singing.

ASHER
Every head on this hill is counted. Mine too.

ASHER
I want a will of mine own. Under this roof, I have none.

AMOS
Then you wish him dead. Tell me I lie. Brother. Tell me.

Asher can't.

Amos turns back to the wind and throws his forkful high. The chaff goes away down the valley.

AMOS
Ask him with your own mouth. I will speak no word for you.

INT. ELIAB'S HOUSE - DUSK #4#

Supper. One lamp on the low olive-wood table, its smoke turning in the last grey light of the open door. Beyond it the gate lamp, lit again, small in the dusk, moths already moving around it. The whole household eats: hired men at the far end, Miriam at the hearth, the dog under the table. Through the door, dogs bark on the other farms down the valley.

Eliab breaks the bread.

ELIAB
Blessed are you, O LORD our God, King of the world, who brings forth bread from the earth.

MICAH
Sixty measures, master. The tithe is set apart, and the fifth for the store.

ASHER
This house has never seen a bad year.

ELIAB
Because we kept the store in every good one.

ASHER
Count the ewe twice, Micah. She came home on a better beast than the rest.

Eliab laughs out loud. The hired men laugh. Amos eats in silence.

ASHER
Father. Sixty measures, the flock, the vines. What is all of it worth?

AMOS
(low)
Eat, Asher.

ELIAB
Worth? One day it shall be yours and your brother's.

Asher has not broken his bread. He looks at Amos, who chews with his eyes on the table. He looks through the door at the gate lamp. Then he sets the bread down flat on the table, and keeps his hand on it.

FLASH. A dog trotting off across a field with a round of bread in its jaws.

ASHER
Father, give me the portion of your goods that falls to me.

Silence. The hired men stop chewing. Miriam's hand stops on the pot.

Amos sets his bread down, his jaw tight, his eyes on his father.

Eliab looks at Amos first. Amos won't meet his eyes.

Eliab looks down at the bread in his hands, and for a long moment he does not move.

ELIAB
You know what you ask.

Asher holds his father's eyes.

ELIAB
Your portion will be silver, son, and silver runs faster than any man who carries it.

ELIAB
Every morning you chose your bed, and I let you choose it.

Asher looks down.

ELIAB
Now you choose the road.

Nobody at the table moves.

ELIAB
Tomorrow I divide my living. To your brother his part, and to you yours.

AMOS
(quietly)
Write mine if you must, Father. Then keep it. I am going nowhere.

EXT. HILLSIDE ABOVE THE LAKE - SUNSET #4A#

The sun is low over the hills. The olives throw long shadows across the seated people.

Eleazar lifts his voice again, for the whole slope to hear.

ELEAZAR
There is a law for such a son.

ELEAZAR
His father shall bring him to the elders at the gate, and all the men of his city shall stone him with stones, that he die.

His hand finds a loose stone on top of the wall, and rests on it. Beside him, Jotham's stylus touches the wax, and stops.

Hezron does not look up. Tirzah looks at the stones lying all over the hillside, and moves closer to her father.

Jesus tears a barley round and puts half of it into Hezron's hand.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - NIGHT #5#

Eliab fills the clay lamp in its niche in the gatepost and trims the wick. The flame bends in the wind off the hills, and holds.

Amos comes out of the house.

AMOS
Tell him no.

ELIAB
A son is not an ox, Amos, to be kept in a stall.

Eliab turns from the lamp to look at him. Amos is already going in.

Eliab stays with the lamp, looking down the dark road.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - FIRST LIGHT

Eliab sits on the stone bench by the gatepost, a sheet of papyrus on his knee and a reed in his hand, and by the last of the gate lamp he writes slowly and squarely, saying each part aloud. In the house doorway Amos stands, watching.

ELIAB
The upper terraces, the olives, the greater flock. To Amos.

ELIAB
The lower field and the vineyard. To Asher.

Micah has come out with the wax. He holds it, and does not give it.

MICAH
Master, he will sell your father's vines.

ELIAB
Then they are his to sell.

Micah gives him the wax. Eliab softens it at the flame, presses his signet into it, and lifts the ring away. His hand shakes once.

EXT. VILLAGE GATE - MORNING, SOME DAYS LATER #6A#

The gate of the village, its stone arch throwing shade. Three ELDERS sit on the bench inside it. Villagers gather; children sit along the top of the wall. Morning dust hangs in the low light.

NAHUM (60s), broad and grey, has a bronze balance on a cloth and a pouch of stone weights.

Asher arrives with the sealed division under his arm. The murmuring stops. A mother pulls her child back out of his way.

ASHER
(bright)
Good morning, fathers. No one has died. You may smile.

Nobody smiles.

NAHUM
The lower field and the vineyard. Ten shekels.

ASHER
Ten? The vines gave forty jars last year. You trod them yourself.

NAHUM
I trod them with your father thirty years. Your grandfather planted them.

ASHER
Ten shekels for forty jars? At that price I would buy them back from you tomorrow.

A child on the wall laughs. A hand hushes it.

Nahum sets a stone in his pan. The beam swings.

NAHUM
Ten. Take it, or carry your paper home.

Asher looks at the silent elders, the faces along the wall. He turns half away, the papyrus crushed in his fist. He is about to go.

The crowd parts. Eliab has come down the hill. He stands beside his son without looking at him, lifts the stone out of Nahum's pan, and weighs it in his palm.

ELIAB
This stone has never been in your pan, Nahum.

NAHUM
(low, for Eliab alone)
Let him go back up the hill ashamed, and the vines stay in your house.

ELIAB
If he goes, he goes by his own will, at a fair price. Not chased off by a light stone.

ELIAB
Forty jars a year, Nahum. A hundred shekels, and by the true stone.

Nahum holds his old friend's eyes. Then he takes the true stone from his pouch and sets it in the pan. Silver pours into the other pan until the beam hangs level.

An elder writes the deed with a reed. The wax is softened. Asher's hand hangs over the deed for one breath. Then he presses his thumb into the wax.

ELDERS
We are witnesses.

At the edge of the crowd stands Amos. He has seen all of it. He says nothing.

Eliab turns and climbs the hill path alone. The whole village watches him go, a small figure on the long slope.

Asher takes one step after him, and stops.

Asher holds the purse of silver. It is heavy. He can't look at anyone.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DAWN #6B#

A grey jenny stands loaded with two leather bags; the silver is sewn into them. Mist in the valley. Millstones in the house.

FLASH. A purse tipped over an open palm; a single coin falls out.

Miriam presses a round of bread into Asher's hands.

MIRIAM
Eat it slowly. There is no oven on that road.

Eliab lays his hands on his son's head. Asher's eyes close.

ELIAB
The LORD bless you, and keep you.

ELIAB
The LORD make his face to shine upon you, and be gracious to you.

ELIAB
The LORD lift up his countenance upon you, and give you peace.

ELIAB
Be strong and of good courage. The LORD your God is with you wherever you go.

ELIAB
The lamp is lit at every dark.

Asher opens his eyes.

ASHER
Save your oil.

Eliab's face goes still. His hand grips the gatepost.

Asher takes the halter and goes out of the gate. The dog starts after him.

ASHER
Stay. Keep the flock. You always did.

The dog stays, and sits at Eliab's feet.

EXT. THE ROAD - THE BEND - MORNING

Amos catches up with Asher at the bend, his own cloak over his arm.

AMOS
You will be cold in the hills beyond the Jordan.

He holds it out. Asher takes it.

AMOS
Father would want you warm.

A short, rough embrace. Amos holds him stiffly. Over his brother's shoulder he sees the sling wound round Asher's wrist.

AMOS
I plaited that for you when you could barely lift a stone.

ASHER
(trying for a joke)
Keep my ewe fat. I will want her for my wedding.

It dies. Amos says nothing.

Asher goes on down the road with the jenny. Amos stands at the bend and watches until his brother is a speck, and then nothing.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DUSK

Eliab strikes a light with his flint and lights the lamp in the niche, alone. The dog lies at his feet.

He puts his hand flat on the stone beside the flame, and weeps without a sound.

Then he wipes his face with his sleeve and turns to the yard.

ELIAB
Shallum! The oxen have not been watered.

EXT. THE ROAD EAST - MONTAGE #7#

-- The crest of the road at sunrise, Asher and the jenny black against the light.
-- The Jordan at noon: brown water to his knees, the jenny's ears back.
-- The bare plain at evening. Far off, a walled city's lights coming on, one by one.

EXT. KALLIAS'S COURT - NIGHT

A colonnaded court in the city, lit by torches and more lamps than anyone could count. Couches, cups, flutes, drums, many languages. At the far end, the open shrine of Tyche, the city's Fortune: a crowned goddess in stone above a roaring brazier, smoke rising to the stars.

Asher lies on a couch in a garland and the purple-hemmed tunic, the city's colour all around him.

KALLIAS (50s), Greek, rings on both hands, a wax tablet and stylus at his elbow, fingers the hem of Asher's tunic.

KALLIAS
Purple at the hem, as a lord's son wears it. And a lord's son counts nothing.

KALLIAS
Whatever name you bore at home, here you shall be Eutychos, the fortunate. A name costs nothing. All else, you shall pay for.

ASHER
(laughing)
Then I am fortunate! Fill every cup!

PHILON (30s), loud, already drunk, raises his.

PHILON
Buy me twice, Eutychos! I am the cheapest friend at this table.

Asher throws an arm around Philon's neck.

ASHER
You are more a brother to me than my brother ever was.

PHILON
Then your brother drinks again, and you pay!

The table roars. Asher turns to Kallias.

ASHER
What does my brother owe you?

Kallias turns the tablet toward him: a long column under Philon's name. Asher takes silver from his purse and drops it on the wax. Kallias draws a line through Philon's column and writes the sum under Asher's. Philon kisses him on both cheeks.

Asher looks up at the colonnade, at the lamps, and throws a handful of silver to the servants.

ASHER
More lamps! Light them all!

Servants run with tapers. The court blazes brighter. At Asher's elbow, Kallias writes.

KALLIAS
Your father must hold half the hills, to send forth such a son.

ASHER
Half the hills he held, until I asked for my half.

KALLIAS
And your brother? If evil found you here, would he come for you?

ASHER
I have no father now. No brother.

Kallias writes it on the tablet.

A priest at the brazier holds out a brimming cup. Asher takes it and, laughing, pours the first of it on the coals before the goddess. The flame leaps. The court cheers.

A woman crosses the floor in dark dyed linen, unhurried. Men look and look away.

KALLIAS
A merchant's wife. Her goodman trades in Sidon, and comes home with the new moon. She is not bought. She chooses.

The woman in dark linen, JES (30s), sits down beside Asher.

JES
They say you asked your father for your share while he still breathes.

ASHER
News travels faster than my jenny.

JES
Every man in this court waits for his father to die. You alone had the courage to say it to his face.

Something crosses Asher's face. Then he smiles, and takes the cup she offers.

He shakes silver from his purse for the next jar. It pours across the table in the torchlight, over his purple hem, and Jes laughs.

EXT. KALLIAS'S COURT - DAWN

Grey dawn on the couches. Bodies asleep. A hot wind out of the east lifts the ash from the goddess's brazier. A wine stain spreads across Asher's purple hem. He stirs. Jes's hand lies on his chest.

ASHER
(half asleep)
At home there is a lamp at the gate. He lights it at every dark.

JES
Sleep. Here, no one wakes you.

He sleeps.

EXT. KALLIAS'S COURT - GATE - NIGHT

Another night. The company spills out, Asher among them. A BLIND BEGGAR sits by the gate, his bowl empty, his hand out.

BLIND BEGGAR
A coin for the blind, young master.

ASHER
Ask my father. He gives to any man who asks.

Philon howls. They pass. The beggar's empty hand stays out.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - MONTAGE OF SEASONS #8#

-- The wheat comes in on the floor; Amos at the flail among the hired men.
-- The first rain darkening the stone around the gate lamp.
-- Rain on the fold; Amos forking extra barley to the ewe with the torn ear.
-- Winter; Eliab's breath showing as he lights the lamp.

EXT. CITY SQUARE BY THE WEST GATE - DUSK, MONTHS LATER

Inside the west gate a caravan is loading for an early start: camels kneeling, bales roped, drivers calling in three tongues. The hot east wind pulls at a cook-fire. Up the street, servants are lighting the city's lamps one by one.

Asher crosses the square with Philon, both in fresh garlands, Jes on Asher's arm, on their way to the night's feast. His purple hem has gone dull with stains. At the edge of the caravan, roping a bale onto his kneeling camel, is the traveller from the village fire. He looks at the garland and the purple hem, and knows him.

TRAVELLER
Eliab's boy.

PHILON
This is Eutychos, old man.

TRAVELLER
The east wind has not stopped in forty days. At first light I go west, by your father's valley. My camel will carry two.

Asher's hand goes to his purse. He weighs it in his palm. It is light.

He looks up the street at the lamps being lit.

ASHER
Tell him the lamps here burn all night.

He walks on toward the lamps, Jes on his arm. Philon throws an arm round his neck. The traveller watches them go, and pulls the rope tight.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - CALF PEN - DAY

A red calf in a pen, its coat glossy. Eliab and SHALLUM (40s), the foreman, at the rail.

ELIAB
Keep a calf on the barley. Always one.

SHALLUM
What feast, master?

ELIAB
The one I am keeping it for.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DAY

The traveller waters his camel at the trough, talking to the hired men. Amos works within earshot.

TRAVELLER
Eliab's boy? I saw him in the city on the plain. Spends like a lord. A woman on each arm. Purple at the hem.

Amos hears it. His face closes.

Eliab has come to the gate behind them. The talk stops. The traveller wipes his mouth.

TRAVELLER
He sends you word, master. The lamps there burn all night.

Eliab looks down the road.

ELIAB
Give him bread before he goes on.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DUSK

Eliab lights the lamp. The dog lies facing the road. Amos stands behind them.

AMOS
Your son is not coming back.

Eliab keeps his eyes on the road.

AMOS
It is written, the elder shall serve the younger.

ELIAB
Read on, son. When Jacob came home, Esau ran to meet him, and fell upon his neck, and kissed him.

AMOS
You never once let me sleep.

ELIAB
When you were small, there was no one else to wake. Then there was.

AMOS
I asked you once. For a kid, for my friends. I was ten.

ELIAB
We had four goats, and a winter coming.

AMOS
I never asked again.

Eliab turns. Amos is already walking away toward the fold.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - FOLD - NIGHT

A drum below in the village, and fires. JOEL (30s), a neighbour, leans on the fold wall.

JOEL
My sister is married tonight. Come down and make merry with your friends.

AMOS
The last of the wheat comes in tonight.

JOEL
The wheat will keep. I will keep you a place.

Joel goes back down the hill. Amos looks into the fold, at a young black kid butting its mother. He looks at it a long time.

Eliab comes to the wall.

ELIAB
The wheat is in. The drum has called you since sundown. Go down to them, son. Take what you will from the fold.

Amos hesitates. He does not look at the fold again. Then he goes, empty-handed, down toward the fires.

EXT. KALLIAS'S COURT - NIGHT #9#

A thin new moon over the colonnade, and the lamps burning along it. Asher's purple hem is stained and fraying. Jes's couch is empty. The wine-seller holds out his palm. Asher tips his purse over it, and a single coin falls out.

Across the room Kallias sees it. He writes one more line on his tablet, and draws a stroke under the column.

At the gate, Jes goes out on the arm of a man with the dust of the road still on his cloak. She does not look back.

Asher looks for Philon. Philon is at another man's elbow, laughing. Asher goes to him.

ASHER
Philon. A coin, for the room.

PHILON
Ask your father. He gives to any man who asks.

The men round him laugh. Philon turns back to his new friend.

INT. RENTED ROOM - DAY

Noon light through a shutter. Asher wakes alone on a stripped bed. The LANDLORD stands in the door.

LANDLORD
It is the new moon. No silver, no room. It is let from tonight.

ASHER
One more moon. I will have it.

LANDLORD
The bags stay, and the jenny, for what you owe.

EXT. CITY MARKET - DAY

A hot, dry east wind drives grit along the street. Stalls are shuttered; one shutter bangs and bangs. Mothers with children wait at an empty grain stall. Flies. No millstones anywhere.

Asher, in his tunic, lays Amos's cloak on a MERCHANT's board.

MERCHANT
One.

Asher takes the copper. He takes off his sandals.

MERCHANT
You will want bread before you want shoes. One.

He unbuckles his belt, the empty purse still on it, and lays it on the board.

The merchant's eyes go to the sling, wrapped around Asher's fist.

MERCHANT
That too.

ASHER
No.

MERCHANT
Keep it, then. You cannot eat it.

EXT. KALLIAS'S COURT - GATE - NIGHT

Asher sits barefoot beside Kallias's gate, in the beggar's corner. His tunic has gone grey, the purple hem frayed to threads, the sling wrapped around his fist. Above the wall, the court's lamps are burning.

Lamps come down the street. Music. Laughter.

PHILON (O.S.)
Eutychos!

Asher's head comes up. For a heartbeat he turns toward the voice.

The company passes: a NEW BOY in a garland, rich and laughing; Philon raising a cup to him; Kallias leaning in at his ear.

KALLIAS
Your father must hold half the hills, to send forth such a son.

And Jes, her hand on the boy's arm.

Asher pulls back into the beggar's corner and covers his face with his sleeve. They pass within arm's reach, in through the gate. Only Jes sees him. She looks away.

He watches them for a few moments: the garland, the cup, Jes's hand. The gate shuts. Laughter behind the wall, and the dark comes back.

EXT. CITIZEN'S ESTATE - DAY #10#

A walled estate on a dry plain. Men wait against the wall for work. The CITIZEN walks the line and looks them over.

ASHER
I know vines. Every spring I tied my father's.

CITIZEN
Look at your hands. Go to my fields, and feed the pigs.

ASHER
I am a Judean. I do not keep pigs.

CITIZEN
It is the pigs, or it is the road. Decide.

Asher stands there. Then he takes the stick the citizen holds out.

EXT. PIG FIELDS - BEFORE DAWN

The citizen's foot in Asher's ribs. Asher wakes on the ground beside the troughs.

CITIZEN
Up. Thirty head. If one is lost, you pay for it.

EXT. PIG FIELDS - DAY

Dark, bristly pigs root in the dust. Flies hang over the troughs. A dry wind crosses the bare field. Carob husks in the trough. Asher's hand drifts toward them.

A HERDSMAN eats in the shade with his small CHILD, who holds a piece of bread.

CHILD
Father. The man by the trough is looking at my bread.

HERDSMAN
Turn your back, then, and eat it. He is the master's to feed. Not ours.

The child turns its back. Asher holds out his open hand to the next man who passes, the way the beggar held out his. Nothing is put in it.

EXT. PIG FIELDS - DUSK

One pig wanders off into the scrub. Asher half rises.

CITIZEN
Let it go. It comes out of your wage.

Asher looks at the dark where the pig went, and gets up, and goes after it.

EXT. SCRUB BEYOND THE PIG FIELDS - NIGHT

Thorns in the dark. He finds the pig caught in them, squealing. He tears it loose, his arms bleeding, and hauls it up across his shoulders, its legs in his fists.

He carries it back through the dark, a Judean with a pig on his back, its filth running down his neck.

EXT. PIG FIELDS - NIGHT

He drops it into the pen. The citizen doesn't look up from his fire.

CITIZEN
I told you to let it go.

Nobody thanks him. Asher stands in the dark with the muck of it on his shoulders.

FLASH. A lamp coming up a black hill; a ewe across his father's shoulders; a man singing under his breath.

He sits down by the trough. The wind. The pigs. He looks up at the stars.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - NIGHT

Far away, Eliab stands at the gate lamp, the flame just caught, looking east down the dark road at the same stars.

EXT. PIG FIELDS - NIGHT

Asher, alone at the trough. He speaks to no one.

ASHER
How many of my father's hired men have bread enough and to spare,

ASHER
and here I perish with hunger!

ASHER
I will arise and go to my father,

ASHER
and I will say to him, Father, I have sinned against heaven, and before you,

ASHER
and I am no longer worthy to be called your son.

ASHER
Make me as one of your hired men.

EXT. HILLSIDE ABOVE THE LAKE - DUSK #11#

A small fire on the ground among the seated people. Lamps in hands. The lake is the colour of pewter.

ELEAZAR
After the swine, he goes home? No father in Israel would open his gate to him.

ELEAZAR
Write it down, Jotham. The elders will want every word.

JOTHAM
I have written nothing.

He looks at the old lines already cut in the wax, and smooths them flat with his thumb.

JOTHAM
Look at the publican.

Hezron weeps into his hands. Tirzah pulls one of his hands away from his face and holds it.

TIRZAH
Abba.

Jesus waits until Hezron's fingers close around his daughter's. Then he goes on, to Hezron now as much as to the gate.

JESUS
And he arose, and came to his father.

EXT. THE ROAD WEST - DAY #12#

Asher walks barefoot on a stony road, in the grey tunic, the sling in his fist. He passes a well where women draw water; nobody looks up. His lips move as he rehearses.

ASHER
Father, I have sinned against heaven, and before you...

ASHER
Make me as one of your hired men.

EXT. A FARM ON THE ROAD WEST - MORNING

A field wall by the road, a single tamarisk over the ditch, and behind the wall a farm in a green fold of the hills, watered by its own spring: the only green thing in the dead brown land. Harvest. Reapers move along the last standing barley, singing. Doves on the roof. Smoke from the bread oven rises straight up.

Under a vine, at a long table, the family sits down to eat: the FATHER (50s), broad, rings on his hands; the MOTHER (40s), laughing, her sleeves rolled; the elder son; and the younger son. By the house door, in a niche in the wall, a little clay god with a lamp burning before it.

The father lifts a loaf and breaks it.

FATHER
To the gods of this house, the first of the bread.

He lays the first piece in the niche before the clay god. The second he gives to his younger son, and his hand rests a moment on the young man's head.

MOTHER
Eat, before your brother eats it all.

A MAID (30s), floured to the elbows, lifts the heavy bar off the pantry door, carries in a board of loaves, and goes back to the oven. She leaves the door open.

Asher stands at the field wall under the tamarisk, barefoot, the sling in his fist. He watches the father's hand on the younger son's head. Then he looks at the open pantry door.

A crash in the pantry. A jar shatters.

MAID
Thief! Thief!

Asher bursts out of the pantry door into the sunlight, a round of bread clutched to his chest, and runs: across the yard through scattering hens, out over the stubble, the elder son close behind, the hired men fanning out.

EXT. EDGE OF THE FIELD - CONTINUOUS

They drag him out of the standing barley to the wall. The elder son wrenches the bread out of his arms and tosses it to the dog, and the dog trots off with it.

ELDER SON
You would steal from my father's house?

The hired men stand round him, breathing hard. One looks at the ribs showing through the rags, and looks away.

ELDER SON
The law says a thief pays it back. But this is a famine, and in a famine I say the thief dies!

He pulls a stone from the wall and weighs it in his palm.

EXT. A FARM ON THE ROAD WEST - THE WELL - LATER

The maid pours water over the men's hands from a jar, slowly, as is the custom before a meal, and it runs red into the dry ground. The two brothers sit down again at the table under the vine. The father lifts the bread.

CUT TO:

EXT. FOOT OF THE SLOPE - THORN SCRUB - BEFORE DAWN

The last stars, and a grey line over the eastern hills. Wind in the thorns. Dew lies on everything: beaded on the thorn branches, on the stones, on his torn rags.

He wakes where he fell, deep in the scrub. He winces at each breath. Dried blood cracks on his face as he opens his eyes.

His breathing slows and steadies. He lies still, a hand on his ribs, and takes the deepest breath the ribs will allow.

He lifts the hand that took the bread, swollen black where the heel came down, and looks at it. He closes it.

He turns his head. The slope above him is empty all the way to the road.

ASHER
(a whisper)
Father?

Only the wind answers.

Dew has gathered on his cracked lips. He wets them with his tongue, and drinks it.

He lies back and watches the last stars go out, one by one, until his eyes close. His chest rises and falls, slow and even.

EXT. THE ROAD WEST - DAY

He walks. He limps. Far off, a line of hills. He stops and looks at them.

EXT. THE ROAD HOME - DUSK

The road climbs, and the valley of his father's farm opens below. Sheep bells rise from it, then a boy's whistle, and a dog's answering bark. The light is going, and the sun is setting straight ahead of him, over his father's hills.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DUSK #13#

Eliab gives the evening orders across the yard. Hired men coming in, oxen, smoke from the ovens.

He goes to the niche and strikes a light for the lamp. As the flame catches, he looks down the road.

Far off, a figure, barefoot and limping, and still walking the way only one person walks.

Eliab drops the flint. He runs.

JESUS (V.O.)
(over the run)
But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him.

An old man, his mantle hitched up in his fist, runs out of his own gate and down the long road in front of his servants, the dog bounding at his heels. The servants stare, then follow. Below, at the village gate, the old men on the bench get to their feet. The whole village watches him run.

EXT. THE ROAD - DUSK

Asher looks up the road. Against the low sun a man is coming toward him, running: black, faceless, a shape and nothing more.

Asher stops dead.

The shape comes on, and as it reaches him the face comes out of the light: old, wet with tears, his father's.

Eliab falls on his neck, and kisses him. The grip finds the cracked ribs and Asher gasps; his father does not let go. Asher stands stiff in his arms, and then he doesn't.

Eliab takes his son's face in his hands and sees the split brow, the blood dried black in it.

ELIAB
Asher.

ASHER
Father, I have sinned against heaven, and in your sight, and I am no longer worthy to be called your son.

Before he can say the rest, Eliab turns to the servants running down the road behind him: Micah, Dan, Shallum, Miriam.

ELIAB
Bring out the best robe, and put it on him;

Micah turns back at a run. Eliab twists the signet off his own finger and presses it into Micah's palm as he goes.

ELIAB
and put a ring on his hand, and shoes on his feet.

Dan runs.

ELIAB
And bring the fatted calf, and kill it; and let us eat, and make merry.

Shallum runs.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - COURTYARD - NIGHT

Torches. The household gathering, neighbours coming up the hill with lamps. Micah pulls the best robe over Asher's head; it is too long in the sleeve. He works the ring over the swollen knuckles of the hand that took the bread.

Miriam kneels with a basin of warm water and takes his cut, filthy feet in her hands. The water goes dark.

MIRIAM
(through tears)
You are late, Asher.

Dan kneels with the sandals.

Eliab turns to the whole yard.

ELIAB
For this my son was dead, and lives again; he was lost, and is found.

The drum starts. Then the pipe. Then everyone.

INT. ELIAB'S HOUSE - NIGHT #14#

The room is full: music and dancing, bread stacked high, the fires roaring, dust shaking up from the stamping feet. Asher sits at the table in the robe. His bread lies untouched. Under the table the dog lies across his feet and will not move.

ASHER
Let me earn it, Father. Put me with the hired men.

ELIAB
You are my son. Eat.

Asher turns to Micah.

ASHER
When does the flock go out?

MICAH
At first light, as it always has.

ASHER
Then I will be at the fold.

Micah looks at him, and nods.

MICAH
Your ewe is there. Your brother has kept her fat.

Asher looks at the door. Amos is not in the room.

EXT. THE FIELD - NIGHT #15#

Amos unyokes the oxen by moonlight in the far field. Up the hill, music. He stops with the yoke in his hands.

Dan comes running down the path with a torch.

AMOS
Dan. What is this?

DAN
Your brother is come; and your father has killed the fatted calf, because he has him back safe and sound.

Amos stands very still.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - NIGHT

Amos stops at the edge of the lamp's light. Through the open house door: the dancing, the fires, the robe. He doesn't take another step.

INT. ELIAB'S HOUSE - CONTINUOUS

Dan pushes through the dancers to Eliab.

DAN
He will not come in.

Asher rises.

ASHER
Let me go out to him.

ELIAB
Stay. I will bring your brother in.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - CONTINUOUS

Eliab comes out to Amos. Warm light behind the father; the cold night behind the son.

ELIAB
Come in, son. Your place is set beside mine.

AMOS
You stood at the gate for him.

AMOS
You made Nahum pay him full weight for our grandfather's vines.

Amos's eyes go to his father's bare finger.

AMOS
Is the ring on his hand?

ELIAB
It is.

AMOS
Many years have I laboured in your service, Father, and never once have I turned from your word.

AMOS
Yet not one kid of the flock was ever given me, that I might make merry with my friends.

Behind them, in the house, the music falters and stops. Faces turn toward the door.

AMOS
But now this son of yours is come, who has wasted your substance among harlots,

In the doorway behind his father, Asher, in the robe, flinches at the word, and does not move.

AMOS
and for him you have slain the fatted calf!

Eliab does not step back. He takes Amos's hand in both of his.

ELIAB
Son, you are ever with me, and all that is mine is yours.

ELIAB
Yet it was fitting that we should make merry and be glad.

Amos pulls his hand free.

ELIAB
For this brother of yours was dead, and lives again. He was lost, and is found.

In the doorway, Asher holds out the sling his brother made him.

Amos looks at it. His jaw sets. He doesn't move.

Eliab's hands stay open between them.

CUT TO:

EXT. HILLSIDE ABOVE THE LAKE - NIGHT #16#

The fire burning low. Lamps among the seated people. The lake below is dark.

TIRZAH
Did the brother go in?

Jesus says nothing. He looks past the fire to the gate, where the two Pharisees stand at the edge of the light.

Hezron lifts the cord over his head and lays the lead tax seal on the ground in front of him. Tirzah looks at it, and then at him.

Jotham looks at the seal on the ground, at the child holding her father's hand. He sets his tablet down on top of the wall. Then he steps through the gate and sits among them. The shepherd boy shifts along to make room for him.

Hezron looks at him.

Eleazar remains in the dark, his hand on the loose stone.

Jesus takes the last barley round and tears it in two. He holds one half out, past the fire, toward the dark beyond the gate, toward Eleazar. On the wall, Eleazar's hand rests on the stone. Neither hand moves.

>FADE OUT.
